WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.640
One of the things that might help speed up your learning algorithm is to slowly reduce

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.180
your learning rate over time.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:08.540
We call this learning rate decay.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:10.700
Let's see how you can implement this.

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:15.380
Let's start with an example of why you might want to implement learning rate decay.

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:20.180
Suppose you're implementing mini-batch gradient descent with a reasonably small mini-batch.

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:24.320
Maybe your mini-batch has just 64, 128 examples.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:29.760
Then as you iterate, your steps will be a little bit noisy and it will tend toward this

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:34.080
minimum over here, but it won't exactly converge.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:40.280
Your algorithm might just end up wandering around and never really converge because you're

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:46.720
using some fixed value for alpha and there's just some noise in your different mini-batches.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:54.640
But if you were to slowly reduce your learning rate alpha, then during the initial phases,

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:59.260
while your learning rate alpha is still large, you can still have relatively fast learning,

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:06.120
but then as alpha gets smaller, your steps you take will be slower and smaller and so

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:13.080
you end up oscillating in a tighter region around this minimum rather than wandering

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.760
far away even as training goes on and on.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:22.200
The intuition behind slowly reducing alpha is that maybe during the initial steps of learning,

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:29.080
you could afford to take your much bigger steps, but then as learning approaches convergence,

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:33.080
then having a slower learning rate allows you to take smaller steps.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:36.600
So here's how you can implement learning rate decay.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:45.500
Recall that one epoch is one pass through the data.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:55.620
So if you have a training set as follows, maybe break it up into different mini-batches,

00:01:55.620 --> 00:02:01.580
then the first pass through the training set is called the first epoch and then the second

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:05.620
pass is the second epoch and so on.

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:13.300
So one thing you could do is set your learning rate alpha to be equal to 1 over 1 plus a parameter

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:22.460
which is going to be called the decay rate times the epoch num.

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:27.380
And this is going to be times some initial learning rate alpha 0.

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:32.340
Note that the decay rate here becomes another hyper parameter which you might need to tune.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:35.060
So here's a concrete example.

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:42.540
If you take several epochs, so several passes through your data, if alpha 0 is equal to

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:53.500
1 and the decay rate is equal to 1, then doing your first epoch, alpha will be 1 over 1 plus

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:59.940
1 times alpha 0, so your learning rate will be 0.1.

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:04.500
That's just evaluating this formula when the decay rate is equal to 1 and the epoch num

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:05.820
is 1.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:14.020
On the second epoch, your learning rate decays to 0.67, on the third, 0.5, on the fourth,

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:16.020
0.4 and so on.

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:20.180
Feel free to evaluate more of these values yourself and get a sense that as a function

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:28.940
of your epoch number, your learning rate gradually decreases according to this formula up on

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:29.940
top.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:35.820
So if you wish to use learning rate decay, what you can do is try a variety of values

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:42.060
of both hyperparameter alpha 0 as well as of this decay rate hyperparameter and then

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:44.780
try to find a value that works well.

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:48.540
Other than this formula for learning rate decay, there are a few other ways that people

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:49.540
use.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:57.260
For example, this is called exponential decay where alpha is equal to some number less than

00:03:57.260 --> 00:04:04.660
1, such as 0.95 times epoch num times alpha 0.

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:10.500
So this will exponentially quickly decay your learning rate.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:18.260
Other formulas that people use are things like alpha equals some constant over epoch

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:27.660
num square root times alpha 0 or some constant K, another hyperparameter, over the

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:33.020
mini-bash number T square rooted times alpha 0.

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:38.340
And sometimes you also see people use a learning rate that decreases in discrete

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:44.020
steps where for some number of steps you have some learning rate and then after a

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:48.460
while you decrease it by one half, after a while by one half, after a while by one

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:49.460
half.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:56.120
And so this is a discrete staircase.

00:04:56.120 --> 00:05:02.720
So so far we've talked about some using some you know formula to govern how alpha

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:05.240
the learning rate changes over time.

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:08.920
One other thing that people sometimes do is manual decay.

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:13.360
And so if you're training just one model at a time and if your model takes many

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:18.360
hours or even many days to train, what some people will do is just watch your model as

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:24.160
it's training over a large number of days and then manually say, oh it looks like the learning

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:27.160
rate slowed down, I'm going to decrease alpha a little bit.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:32.160
Of course this works, this manually controlling alpha, really tuning alpha by hand, all by

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:33.760
hour, day by day.

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:37.800
This works only if you're training only a small number of models, but sometimes people

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:39.080
do that as well.

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:43.560
So now you have a few more options for how to control the learning rate alpha.

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:47.560
Now in case you're thinking, wow this is a lot of hyper parameters, how do I select amongst

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:51.200
all these different options, I would say don't worry about it for now.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:56.520
In next week we'll talk more about how to systematically choose hyper parameters.

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:00.680
For me, I would say that learning rate decay is usually lower down on the list

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:02.240
of things I try.

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:05.720
Setting alpha just a fixed value of alpha and getting that to be well tuned has a

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:06.720
huge impact.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:08.840
Learning rate decay does help.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:12.360
Sometimes it can really help speed up training, but it is a little bit lower down

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:15.480
my list in terms of the things I would try.

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:19.320
But next week when we talk about hyper parameter tuning, you see more systematic

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:23.120
ways to organize all of these hyper parameters and how to efficiently search

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:24.320
amongst them.

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:27.560
So that's it for learning rate decay.

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:32.120
Finally, I also want to talk a little bit about local optima and saddle points

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:36.040
in new networks so you can have a little bit better intuition about the types of

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:40.120
optimization problems your optimization algorithm is trying to solve when you're

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:41.720
trying to train these neural networks.

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:43.720
Let's go on to the next video to see that.
